implementationPlan.description:
Adversarial self-review of this task's diff only. Load the adversarial-self-review skill, then read the diff as each attacker in turn:
- A hostile host: frames split byte by byte; two frames back to back; a 4 GiB length; a slow partial header; extra keys; NUL, newline or non-ASCII in the value; a cli or role trying to reach argv.
- A hostile agent: reading GUARD's environ or cmdline; signalling GUARD; pre-planting the sessions dir or a workspace symlink; expecting a reused HOME.
- The environment: no moe-agent account; a missing sessions parent; a non-root or root runner; macOS, where J1-J3 must pass and J4 skips.

Check that:
- the error handling on every refusal writes no byte to stdout;
- the credential is in no argv, file, log or exception message;
- 78 and EX_CONFIG are gone from the bridge path and the header;
- job=None leaves every legacy relay() caller unchanged;
- verified-local, executionReady and every isolation claim are untouched;
- there is no .moe edit and no Git write.

Mutation spot-check in a scratch `git archive HEAD | tar -x` copy, never in the shared tree. Score each mutant by exit code with --test-name-pattern='^(J[1-4]|S7) ' and wrap each run in timeout. Mutants and the test that should kill each:
- over-reading the header or the body: J2, J3;
- the length check after the body read: J1;
- a superset key check: J1;
- dropping `type(cli) is str`: J1;
- CREDENTIAL {1,4095}: J2; match() instead of fullmatch(): J1;
- stat() for the workspace: J1;
- dropping the sessions mode check: J3;
- extra_groups None by default: J3;
- an inherited env: J4;
- GUARD without NNP, or ignoring the spawn keywords: J4.
No test can force the prctl failure branch, since Linux 3.5+ never refuses the call. Say so in the step note rather than adding a seam.

Known residual, to report and not fix here: earlier homes stay moe-agent-owned. A later job that learns one's name (0711 stops it listing them) could enter it, and artifact handling belongs to R10 final.

Fix anything found with a test, then re-run step 3's command and supply that fresh output to complete_task.

implementationPlan.note:
Adversarial review of this task's diff, done.

Hostile host:
- a byte-by-byte split works (read_exact loops);
- a second frame is left for relay, where it is a protocol violation (76);
- a 4 GiB length is refused before any body read;
- extra keys, NUL, newline, DEL and non-ASCII values are refused with fixed text;
- duplicate JSON keys resolve last-wins and are then fully validated;
- argv is jobs[cli][0] only.

Hostile agent:
- GUARD is root, so the agent cannot read its environ or signal it; GUARD's cmdline carries only {user, group, extra_groups, umask, cwd};
- sessions must be euid-owned with no g/o write, and lstat refuses symlinks; the workspace check uses lstat, and its root-owned parent stops a swap;
- every HOME comes fresh from mkdtemp.

Environment:
- no account: 71;
- a missing workspace gives 66 before sessions is ever created;
- root runner: J4's EPERM row is skipped and the uid+1 row still gives 66;
- macOS: J1-J3 use no /proc and J4 skips.

Every refusal writes nothing to stdout or stderr (J1 and J3 assert both empty). No 78 or EX_CONFIG remains anywhere in scripts, docs or the daemon. job=None runs GUARD with '{}' and env=None, so legacy behaviour is unchanged apart from GUARD's NNP bit; all B/S/I/H tests pass. No verified-local, executionReady or isolation claim was touched, and there were no .moe edits or Git writes.

Mutation sweep (/tmp/r10b-mut-tools/mutate.py with mutants{,2}.json; a git-archive HEAD scratch copy plus my 2 files; `^(J[1-4]|S7) `; scored by exit code):
- 43/44 killed. Among them: over-read, length check after the body, superset keys (both levels), no type(cli), no type(version), CREDENTIAL {1,4095}, {1,4097}, {0,..}, \x20 and \x7f, match(), EOF as 65, stat() for the workspace, home before workspace, sessions mode (3 variants), sessions stat(), 0755, a reused home, groups None, umask, cwd, job positional, inherited and merged env, no NNP, no spawn keywords, and main 78.
- Survivor, equivalent: sessions without S_ISDIR. A Linux symlink is mode 0777, so the write-bit check refuses it, and makedirs raises on any other non-directory. Kept as defense in depth, like Bridge.open.
- Fix found by the sweep: dropping os.chown(home) survived, because a non-root owner makes it a no-op. J3 now wraps os.chown to record its calls and asserts each job's exact (home, uid, gid) call; the 'no chown' and 'chown sessions' mutants are killed.

Not testable by design: the prctl failure branch (Linux 3.5+ never refuses it), and the euid-owner check on sessions when running non-root.

Residuals, for R10 final:
1. Earlier homes stay moe-agent-owned; 0711 stops listing them, not entering one by a known name.
2. All jobs on one VM share the moe-agent uid, so a process that escapes the group with setsid survives the killpg and can read a later job's /proc environ.
3. A slow partial job header blocks before hello; the host session timeout owns that.

Fresh gate after the fix: exit 0, 290/289/1 skip. Size: +118/-20 supervisor, +152/-1 tests, 291 changed lines.
